## Engineering outcome, 5 October

After the initial ticket submission, the course desk published an update on the
[challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update):
ticket #4471 closed on 5 October. The parser was **fixed** by
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The hint was **won't fix** because the
booking-v3 form was retired. The two ticket closure reasons must preserve that
difference: parser **completed**, hint **not planned**.

The exported reports and computed counts above remain the historical evidence
through 4 October. Retirement is not evidence that the misleading hint was repaired,
and this update does not independently explain each of the five anomalous reports.
The fixes and checks discussed below describe the two failure mechanisms; the desk's
actual resolutions are the ones recorded here.

## Shared parser: day-first dates read month first

For a day-first tenant, the shared parser reads the supplied date month first. The
booking-v2 form, app and email paths fall in this group; booking-v3 app and email
reports do too. For example, R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July. Use the tenant
directory and `Intl.DateTimeFormat` date-part order, not the reporter's browser locale
or intake tag, to identify the affected path.

Fix the parser to respect the declared tenant date order and use an unambiguous date
representation at system boundaries. Test app, email and booking-v2 with a day-first
tenant and ambiguous input such as `07/04/2026`. Test a booking-v3 app report as well,
because the form version alone does not decide the cause.

## Booking-v3 form: hint contradicts the tenant's input order

The booking-v3 date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. On a day-first tenant's form, people
follow that hint, while the form interprets the value in the tenant's order. This is
the hint failure, limited by the source classifier to the form channel on booking-v3.
R-41230, from Copper Studio, is an example even though intake tagged it as parser.
Correct the visible hint to match the actual parsing contract and test that contract
for Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour. A parser-only change does not fix
the misleading instruction on the form.

## What the queue chart means

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Compare received dates with merge dates. Reports existed well before the desk merged
its backlog. The later merges do not demonstrate that the bug started then or was
fixed. The export is a snapshot through 4 October; re-check GitHub issue bodies,
comments and states before submitting, because the desk keeps working.

## Replace subject matching with cause matching

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
instruct the desk to merge on subject alone. They are old enough to trigger the
layout's stale warning. The explorer's executable examples contradict that rule:

<DedupeExplorer />

Matching subjects can hide different causes, even for the same tenant. Different
subjects or channels can describe the same cause. Look up the tenant, locale, form
and channel, then compare the failure mechanism. If the tenant is unknown, leave the
report unplaced and investigate rather than merging on a matching subject.

Decision 0001 remains unchanged because accepted decisions are immutable. Propose a
new decision to supersede it after review by its owners. Keep the five anomalous
reports listed above under explicit investigation; do not silently invent a proven
third cause or count the same report twice.

